      "index": "a9",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1444768722,
      "version": 639,
      "versionNonce": 1387002194,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785224392932,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. A constructor is a special member of a class. It has the same name as the class and has no return type.\n\n2. If it has a return type (even void), it is treated as an instance method, not a constructor.\n\n3. If the programmer does not define any constructor, the Java compiler (javac) automatically\n provides a default constructor during compilation.\n\n4. When the JVM executes a `new` expression, it:\n   - allocates memory for the object,\n   - performs default initialization,\n   - invokes the constructor,\n   - executes explicit field initializations and instance initializer blocks,\n   - executes the constructor body to initialize the object,\n   - and finally returns the object reference.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. A constructor is a special member of a class. It has the same name as the class and has no return type.\n\n2. If it has a return type (even void), it is treated as an instance method, not a constructor.\n\n3. If the programmer does not define any constructor, the Java compiler (javac) automatically\n provides a default constructor during compilation.\n\n4. When the JVM executes a `new` expression, it:\n   - allocates memory for the object,\n   - performs default initialization,\n   - invokes the constructor,\n   - executes explicit field initializations and instance initializer blocks,\n   - executes the constructor body to initialize the object,\n   - and finally returns the object reference.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
